Read the finger once it has arrived, not the moment it is detected.

Host-side finger detection polls the sensor against its background and takes
the first frame over the threshold as the capture. On this device an empty
sensor polls at 4-17 and a contact at 130-206, so that first frame is a
fingertip touching at one edge rather than a finger resting on the sensor.

Measured over 32 live authentications before this change: the single accepted
contact covered all sixteen tiles of the sensor and correlated at 0.864 with
the enrolled references, while the rejections covered as few as two tiles and
correlated between 0.14 and 0.67. Those were not the wrong finger - they were
the right one, caught on its way down.

Three polls 100ms apart must clear the threshold before a contact is accepted,
and the frame kept is the last of them. The four authentications measured
afterwards covered 13-16 tiles and correlated 0.940-0.982.

